# What Angis cannot do yet

Say, Angis can be taught new wording, but it still has real limits.
Say, It only understands phrases the parser knows, or phrases you define with Define phrase, When I say, or Teach Angis.
Say, It is not a full replacement for Python, C, or JavaScript yet.
Say, Complex low-level systems code, drivers, kernels, compilers, advanced networking, and native apps still need host languages or libraries.
Say, Some features depend on installed Python packages like pygame, OpenGL, databases, or other imports.
Say, The Desktop copy and Apps copy are not the same version.
Say, /Users/fellflow/Apps/Angis is newer and supports more natural Teach Angis flow.
Say, It cannot guess every possible sentence.
Say, If wording is too vague, it needs a phrase definition.
Say, It does not have a mature package ecosystem like npm or pip.
Say, It does not automatically know every API unless you bridge or import it.
Say, Visual apps and games are prototype-level, not production game-engine level.
Say, 3D exists, but not like Unity or Unreal.
Say, Browser and web deployment exist in limited form, not a full React or Next.js style web stack.
Say, Security and permissions are basic.
Say, It is not a hardened sandbox.
Say, Error messages can still be rough when a phrase fails.
Say, Refactoring, autocomplete, type checking, debugging, and IDE tooling are not as mature as mainstream languages.
Say, Some phrase-slot edge cases still need fixes as they are found.
Say, The honest limit is that Angis can be taught almost any wording, but not every capability is native yet.
Say, For deep system work, production web stacks, advanced engines, or unknown APIs, it still needs interpreter work or Python library bridges.
Say, Deep systems work, production web stacks, advanced engines, and unknown APIs still need interpreter work or Python/library bridges.
Say, What Angis does not have yet includes a mature package manager, dependency resolver, lockfile workflow, or public package registry.
Say, It does not have full production web framework features like routing, SSR, hydration, bundling, auth, database migrations, or deployment adapters.
Say, It does not have native mobile app builds for iOS or Android.
Say, It does not have mature desktop app packaging for every operating system.
Say, It does not have advanced game engine systems like physics pipelines, animation graphs, asset importers, shaders, networking replication, or editor tooling.
Say, It does not have a full static type checker, language server, autocomplete engine, formatter, or refactoring engine.
Say, It does not have a hardened permission sandbox, capability isolation, code signing flow, or security audit system.
Say, It does not have built-in cloud infrastructure tools for containers, Kubernetes, serverless, CI/CD, secrets management, or observability.
Say, It does not have native drivers, kernels, embedded firmware support, or real-time systems support.
Say, It does not have complete compatibility with every Python library unless that library is installed and bridged.
Say, It does not have guaranteed production-grade performance for large programs yet.
Say, It does not have complete docs, tutorials, or examples for every feature.
